  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does Dorothea marry Casaubon?",
      "a": "She is idealistic and hungry for learning, and imagines that helping a great scholar will give her life purpose. She mistakes his dryness for depth.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did Dorothea choose Casaubon?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the Key to All Mythologies?",
      "a": "Casaubon's unfinished life work, meant to trace all myths to one source. Will Ladislaw points out it is out of date because Casaubon cannot read the German scholars, and it dies with him.",
      "asks": [
        "What is Casaubon writing?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does Lydgate fail?",
      "a": "His marriage to the expensive Rosamond drives him into debt, and his loan from Bulstrode ties him to the banker's scandal. He ends as a fashionable doctor, his reforming dream given up.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens to Lydgate in Middlemarch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is Middlemarch about?",
      "a": "Subtitled A Study of Provincial Life, it follows several interwoven lives in a Midlands town around the Reform Act of 1832, especially marriages, vocations and money, showing how individuals are shaped by their community.",
      "asks": [
        "Middlemarch summary"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who was George Eliot?",
      "a": "The pen name of Mary Ann Evans (1819 to 1880), a translator, editor and novelist who used a male name so her work would be taken seriously, and because she lived openly with the married George Henry Lewes.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did George Eliot use a male name?"
      ]
    }
  ],
